#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151861 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "260712d9-b48d-552e-a31a-0c2e74cafeb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6241" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0ad490af48e1db93ab9cae7e482b5eb8956aa14f8d2972816f8852179b35c4cb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a33927647d7651ce47c9e6619bd26a29ceb5cc5dd74e379bf81ea61806fb5c95.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=2+3+4+5=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "07269d0a-faf3-5bf9-b397-8fc1574313a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6242" :question/problem "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=15 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=10 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=12 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151968 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/id #uuid "60ec2df0-87b9-544f-b286-2ed5e17d92fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9476" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29cc7d83-a830-5a1c-8e3c-218d8330e430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374690} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd07bf3-bb8c-534f-a5fe-e8290031d9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374691} {:answer/id #uuid "fba0251e-6617-5bd9-b2c8-733531c56745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374692} {:answer/id #uuid "ba59ca59-d1a7-5a0e-89ab-7fd784e8f038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374693} {:answer/id #uuid "10754718-8327-598b-ae0a-fc17db8b4edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{12}\\cdot {m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba0251e-6617-5bd9-b2c8-733531c56745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374692} :answer-field/id #uuid "e396a8d4-801c-5f9b-9738-7747e0d05163" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fc86049-64cc-5cd7-9cd9-a6d9803210f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122876" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0abae6be42361d157d7cbd46f602d3a0d2044cfec551c61a2739ff9b56798cc3.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{12}+3\\cdot {m}_{13} \\\\  & =3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e31f8121-db80-5805-9fb8-8505ab321f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374697} {:answer/id #uuid "0efb8953-1ee4-5cfc-a4ec-61521700088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374698} {:answer/id #uuid "ef197b52-59c2-5324-afec-d0586485263a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374699} {:answer/id #uuid "19880e16-b56c-550a-b336-efb7f9c47056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}-{m}_{12}-{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374700} {:answer/id #uuid "7ced1df6-1105-5a02-86cc-9e039dcfb3d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{12}\\cdot {m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0efb8953-1ee4-5cfc-a4ec-61521700088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374698} :answer-field/id #uuid "7989e358-fb6b-505f-bf67-0f76c203985f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "506002bd-7e91-53f8-9a50-717c484bb8df" :question/math-academy-id "q-122877" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/954a3a59a167bd14457d57541e2747f612c5c84ae45cf6b3dc23a534cf354845.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\cdot {m}_{11}+2\\cdot {m}_{12}+2\\cdot {m}_{13} \\\\  & =2({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcce5f3a-605d-5233-8276-ee4bf915d1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374704} {:answer/id #uuid "da32a5e1-bac4-562f-91b6-f17ecfee3652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{21}\\cdot {m}_{12}\\cdot {m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374705} {:answer/id #uuid "91e40e31-4391-555c-824d-8e5abe3c3f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}-{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}-{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374706} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc0c968-977e-5ce8-9ebc-3baccc68af3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}-{m}_{21}-{m}_{12}-{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374707} {:answer/id #uuid "98401dcc-4974-54bf-b1f3-032e17a44735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98401dcc-4974-54bf-b1f3-032e17a44735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374708} :answer-field/id #uuid "636d0a04-0bc2-5269-9b48-6d5acd90fffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14429180-581e-5e34-8afc-a6b3c464de1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122878" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/be2c8a433cbd5b812e8c02c5da6b8d2e713ffa8b21dfb327f553a09df8743a82.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+1\\cdot {m}_{21}+1\\cdot {m}_{12}+1\\cdot {m}_{22} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215f7fee-3c83-52f5-83d9-1ae95e59497d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374711} {:answer/id #uuid "8236eabb-f32b-5be6-834e-b7135a2d5788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374712} {:answer/id #uuid "0efed331-a464-51ea-823c-391290237da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+4{m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374713} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce3d969-603d-50ab-8288-0b83a2d777df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+4{m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374714} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf31e26-5e67-5578-821e-db819e868d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf31e26-5e67-5578-821e-db819e868d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374715} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e93948-1da7-5dff-958b-3416ca860b3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f07abbfb-88c5-593d-ab7d-6acdce993b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-122879" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/dca2037c7f7b5e45fca3268e4114dea0e939a6c08d02440fb1363f602f263273.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\cdot {m}_{11}+4\\cdot {m}_{21}+2\\cdot {m}_{31} \\\\  & =2{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f25af93-19c4-53a6-840d-d3fb448437f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374718} {:answer/id #uuid "4752ec09-94c3-532d-adee-7eaaff587eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374719} {:answer/id #uuid "76a9edc5-4f3f-5257-bb6a-3420efabeea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374720} {:answer/id #uuid "afb5f180-6452-5f5a-81bf-3929cee51c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374721} {:answer/id #uuid "37341659-441c-5060-a470-f7b80820726e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb5f180-6452-5f5a-81bf-3929cee51c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374721} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bd6b56a-f465-5a8c-bd4c-d2c5313f52dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75da9d4f-8463-5d83-bf73-1d9757147503" :question/math-academy-id "q-122887" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/456aefd5e7fd85024c6d410d8b01bf3a49919504bb0023373bd0e94e768f584f.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+2\\cdot {m}_{21}+1\\cdot {m}_{12}+2\\cdot {m}_{22} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a571258a-151f-50a1-9e9d-defb1c8b1ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374725} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf83406-0d95-5538-9ece-894c626909f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374726} {:answer/id #uuid "b65a9b47-33f2-588c-8ef8-d1b841b6d3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374727} {:answer/id #uuid "b02258d2-33c1-5645-a155-8e995cf7defa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374728} {:answer/id #uuid "6d58d551-c280-5af8-a377-ac83a57e9324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a571258a-151f-50a1-9e9d-defb1c8b1ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374725} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d9efe85-2419-525e-8745-a91a2e90612f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "931005fb-fa0f-5296-b05d-7a62683dab17" :question/math-academy-id "q-122961" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=13-x-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2,3,4},\\,{P}_{2}={1,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/48356d566752c064e9381c2f9ce27ef12bbdd86a29a2dab0d3dda05088630006.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})+{m}_{41}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{41})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}+2{m}_{41}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/90ed44b97d085413780f2dce297dd78ffa4c968a244e73c431d2e9aa30e14d22.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/860ba8cf7e5135f7e2eedcde7534fe9880feb38c29163625e32b79f18fce2f2f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,3)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,3)=1 \\\\ {m}_{41} & =f(4,3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337577f0-587c-56f9-9cd6-2dc121ed0024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374732} {:answer/id #uuid "3880fb60-00c1-546b-85d1-b96a4bae7f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374733} {:answer/id #uuid "534b5fac-a446-5b5a-8084-1e54f72a5e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374734} {:answer/id #uuid "46dbf4c9-3214-5c5d-afac-3d6d18680b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374735} {:answer/id #uuid "f69d30be-0cb3-5e61-b9cb-a7c731057174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "337577f0-587c-56f9-9cd6-2dc121ed0024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374732} :answer-field/id #uuid "078b6ad6-05f8-557a-bfea-7ca2f7afb86a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d5aa743-e702-5060-b731-9d863fad5c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-122963" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-2x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/40eb21cbafdbd721ac8f1710d52918d5e463fa3ffc6eb06aeba8539eb53c7db8.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-2x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-2x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/c17b413125919726cd1aba9efdc4dcb5949fee52947610abcbb952f1b7917281.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/5dbf341642f5e15942323ec3d5674fc361f0b48d9cabd5f54c7fedb95a3f6fb6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(3,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,3)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7452d5-0705-581b-a59b-b1bece519fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374739} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6b4526-915a-5012-aeac-c2de3561af7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374740} {:answer/id #uuid "246976f1-6408-5802-8089-990861dc4c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374741} {:answer/id #uuid "d441950e-023b-5fc5-ad92-13a35f64f53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374742} {:answer/id #uuid "c20b7b90-5836-5940-8766-87a215cfeab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e7452d5-0705-581b-a59b-b1bece519fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374739} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d76b53-9394-5afb-b842-8f7d61b9f896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a67b2dc-d153-554d-bdc5-81da5d74bfd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122965" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=13-2x-2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-2x-2y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-2x-2y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/52c19da39a43cdbf1b1d2565669c391e59679b3bf9828f7c46755831b2b0890c.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/2acddf617eda8211e45d281329051f08a8b83788e24b70ebe8d526b58b195bba.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,3)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=5+3+3+1=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e265f0bd-ff91-57f9-baac-4d6322cc9b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374746} {:answer/id #uuid "c854148a-cdcd-5ea8-ad3b-1ba7089153e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374747} {:answer/id #uuid "4025005c-099c-5b03-bd85-c8e15b004e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374748} {:answer/id #uuid "ab473f11-4cbd-5c1c-972b-57da88c085ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374749} {:answer/id #uuid "47f9686f-fa22-5a07-94f1-ae7c9af40d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e265f0bd-ff91-57f9-baac-4d6322cc9b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374746} :answer-field/id #uuid "601deace-2b17-5d75-b0b2-1763d24d6366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2dbee57-6abe-5f32-af00-c75c7d933c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-122983" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/7310b1302c35084aaef5a8b2316f59c0fbd806bc9314a51fe77167988d01f343.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/ce58eb1c2ee59cf97aa77fb9e840578e7a99f094b11b2e282317aad69ae31b7a.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/cec70986dea862392342377d83adbfa534ce0f3aa87cf2161c2f88eb83fbed58.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(0,2)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83196706-03f2-5836-a704-5626c84db581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150697} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c05ccf-ae08-5208-b06a-b1f46a231939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150698} {:answer/id #uuid "47b8189b-28f8-53cb-b8a6-ebedf0cdc165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150699} {:answer/id #uuid "64b475d6-5fe9-5903-bc89-1c798dca7762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150700} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff8ce41-0aec-52a1-943b-75a2ab4ac8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47b8189b-28f8-53cb-b8a6-ebedf0cdc165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150699} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d81f07-0384-512f-b5e7-06fb6c89bf6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ff7ee9c-86e4-5538-972f-ae0d4081ca31" :question/math-academy-id "q-75035" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd9c42be-42dd-5a3b-bdd0-975a979b6b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150703} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ab31ee-1191-51bc-8023-b84802fd7d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150704} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ed72f0-34cb-5d55-b0f1-d6b4d466af56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150705} {:answer/id #uuid "30361be7-40da-5595-be74-157b8639889d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150706} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2a6881-ca33-5962-ae82-15d297a95937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd9c42be-42dd-5a3b-bdd0-975a979b6b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150703} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a4966a-287d-57c9-8151-9754df37f1d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6605808-1c43-50ef-9666-dfe55ed838b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75037" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0fefe892c74e8d391e56ee822f83e7e0fe0110593c9d6a4b93b8cfe1e558ab16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/fe872f1f1b0307760dcdba6ac6f6be20f197662ac250ab46dc77fa472295906f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=3+2+2+1=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e71cfc7-c6cf-5882-bd31-ccdeabb5b166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374753} {:answer/id #uuid "68ab3f74-7346-59c0-9a4d-61798056d306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374754} {:answer/id #uuid "f88da270-fb30-5a5f-af31-5813fa7088fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374755} {:answer/id #uuid "677db18a-fcac-54d1-8857-3a467d432ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374756} {:answer/id #uuid "82ef1c3e-09cb-5ad0-bfc7-889b54cb52f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68ab3f74-7346-59c0-9a4d-61798056d306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374754} :answer-field/id #uuid "f914be0b-4a43-5aea-99fa-b140ab7e6dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b45ef0a9-5ca0-51f8-b366-e36372f17811" :question/math-academy-id "q-75040" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/d7e548ae50bc8c7f0e33e8bb43ba8950788f735af103c4923d50d147995a3bb6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/c6bdb6a479878c3fed94923a5db19d5e55db3431b685344d54b9a384f70a60e3.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,0)=11 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=11 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =10+11+11+12 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04e94270-3f31-5041-b7b2-8f4f9de9c7f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374760} {:answer/id #uuid "9016dcf1-237d-5dc8-b22d-41637ce20225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374761} {:answer/id #uuid "ee700184-217c-52e7-8947-7d339944fde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374762} {:answer/id #uuid "27b62375-2fe8-52ac-a3bd-d9e0177ae998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374763} {:answer/id #uuid "2957a788-3e6b-5297-bc65-d24b39c46db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9016dcf1-237d-5dc8-b22d-41637ce20225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374761} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0ede59-6fc5-561a-a8db-bd5cc2df3829" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c690e767-ce40-5114-836c-aee40e57608f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75230" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/9b26ea696afda59a7181f062c7c9fab4be373035678eafa0da51bf2b22f9f4b3.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})+{m}_{23}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $6$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}+{m}_{13}+{m}_{23}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/70e4f9cd213e0dc4429e21cacf5c375d4def1e9ba021f05e45ee6e7820c5ff36.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/8135011b8384a668d4bab28282220bb0560e1ae64c2f1d623822435349067f18.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(1,3)=4 \\\\ {m}_{23} & =f(2,3)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2+3+3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe2372d2-8d7d-51c5-8c84-006d0dafc353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374767} {:answer/id #uuid "c383c7f1-177e-5435-a49a-cf39f18edcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374768} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdf5f93-9023-5100-b545-6c242d092410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374769} {:answer/id #uuid "720e5910-6acd-5e3c-ae73-73c1dd1e07ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374770} {:answer/id #uuid "791fad60-3a39-52a2-88eb-7b72c986a22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "720e5910-6acd-5e3c-ae73-73c1dd1e07ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374770} :answer-field/id #uuid "afd4c7e4-7e14-5574-80f0-31b37c14cae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b715fe4d-32ff-5b40-a10a-915385f0e83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75248" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y-1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/b3c2a3891860a8ad88d2f111a3e279a65398a37020ab2f0843f8475dfd38df15.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/76cbbcf7ddff625cf035d6f7e37acbc6d278734cc87648ef6a88f8ace61f731e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,0)=0 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =0+1+1+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f55e259-c8f7-5dd9-ab39-f4ec97b8cca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374774} {:answer/id #uuid "8b06600e-fc47-5dbc-ad3c-2d55e6fb02f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374775} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2463e0-97e4-5305-9a4a-0194e4387f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374776} {:answer/id #uuid "02f3012a-4bdf-5e92-8b31-dd4f161f1178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374777} {:answer/id #uuid "ce78f1a2-b4a8-53aa-8ea7-8c44a567d711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c2463e0-97e4-5305-9a4a-0194e4387f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374776} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b5e7c3c-8550-5f46-a0a7-338080b27271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f272e6a-b67d-5c18-b295-5bef72a286d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75255" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/e70e2cf07b27a91c34e59435229302f3b2127b15a2f39049c0a8389cd6f59863.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/aa3858dba149ebf5650ed06f75444244494fe0a961f897100914d54f6d7c0b49.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=8 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=8+5+3+0=16.\n$$"}]}
